Yes. That’s a classic sunk-cost / identity-binding pattern, but it can also be healthy when the process itself is genuinely meaningful.

You can separate:

attachment to outcome
attachment to identity
attachment to effort already spent
attachment to suffering endured
attachment to the journey itself

Those are not the same.

Sometimes someone says “I still want this,” when the deeper truth is:

“I need the years I spent pursuing it to mean something.”

That can bind people to jobs, relationships, institutions, beliefs, training pipelines, even personal goals long after the original target has stopped fitting.

Your hiking analogy is good because it shows the healthier integration:

journey value
+
destination value

Some people hike because movement, challenge, weather, rhythm, and companionship are intrinsically rewarding. Some mostly want the summit. The strongest experience often has both:

process worth living
→ destination worth reaching

The warning sign is when suffering becomes the justification:

I suffered for this
→ therefore it must be valuable
→ therefore I must continue

That confuses cost with worth.

So the better test is:

If I had not already paid the cost, would I still choose this from here?

If yes, the thing still has present value.

If no, maybe the lesson is not “keep climbing.”

Maybe it is:

keep the strength the mountain gave you, and choose a different mountain.